// Portable schema migrations end to end: `schema_migrations/` holds two `.schema` files (the schema
// IR) and one `.sql` file (raw SQL) side by side. `conn.migrate(dir)` applies all three in filename
// order through the one engine — the IR bodies lowered to the connected driver's DDL, the SQL body
// run verbatim — and tracks every one of them in `_noeta_migrations` exactly the same way.
//
// **Why `.schema` here and not the usual `.noe`.** A migration is normally a Noeta program returning
// `List<Statement>`, which `noeta migrate` runs. This demo migrates *itself*, from inside a running
// program, and that surface holds a database driver but not the loader a second program would need —
// so its directory is `.sql`/`.schema` only. That is the one place writing the IR by hand still earns
// its keep; a project driven by `noeta migrate` writes Noeta.
//
// The point: this program is dsn-agnostic. Point it at "postgres://…" and the same three files build
// the same schema there, with `BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY` where SQLite got
// `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT`. Run from the repo root:
//
//   noeta run examples/para-db-demo/schema_migrate_demo.noe
use para.db

conn = db.connect("sqlite::memory:") // or "postgres://…" — the migrations do not change

// 1. Apply: a `.schema` create, a raw `.sql` insert, then a `.schema` alter — one ordering.
applied = conn.migrate("examples/para-db-demo/schema_migrations")
echo "applied ${applied} migration(s)"

// 2. Re-running is a no-op: a DSL migration is checksummed and tracked like any other. The checksum
//    is over the canonical form of the schema each file DESCRIBES — not its text, not the DDL — so
//    it is identical on SQLite and PostgreSQL, and reformatting a file is not edited history.
again = conn.migrate("examples/para-db-demo/schema_migrations")
echo "re-run applied ${again} migration(s)"

// 3. The schema the DSL described is live, including the column the alter added.
rows = conn.query("SELECT id, title, pinned, archived FROM notes ORDER BY id", [])
echo "notes -> ${rows.len()}"
for row in rows {
    id = row["id"]
    title = row["title"]
    pinned = row["pinned"]
    archived = row["archived"]
    echo "  #${id} ${title} (pinned=${pinned}, archived=${archived})"
}

// 4. The tracking table records the DSL migrations under their own filenames.
tracked = conn.query("SELECT filename FROM _noeta_migrations ORDER BY filename", [])
for row in tracked {
    name = row["filename"]
    echo "  tracked ${name}"
}

conn.close()
